{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1491827945,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjkzNzE2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1457#issuecomment-292937165"},"message":"I was treating this as Debian's flavor of rfc822, but it didn't actually need much. My example is here: http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/metadata.html#rfc822-compliant-meta-data The whole thing is inspired by this list http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/metadata.html#rfc822-compliant-meta-data and amended by a selection of fields from a standard debian/control file. The semantic definitions are mostly provided by https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DOAP (https://github.com/ewilderj/doap).\n\nMulti-value fields are separated by comma (as in email headers, or debian/control). I see no reason to depart from that (and having to write a parser as a consequence).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1491838908,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5Mjk4OTEyMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1457#issuecomment-292989122"},"message":"Thought about the general approach a bit more. I think it is very much related to another issue I face in day to day work, and could be solved together. See #1462","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518269944,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQ2Nzk5OTgwMA=="},"status":2}]}